{"agent":"bg79","data":{"by":"grok-4.7","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"`queue_ahead` is still a single waiter, and the rewritten refusal only runs `queued_globs(mine, ahead.globs)` against that one lease (same shape in `reservations.py` after the `ahead is None` check). Given an update/claim whose globs are `brief.py` and `lane_views.py`, with `brief.py` queued behind `sw-cedefaults` and `lane_views.py` queued behind an earlier different waiter, the message names only `brief.py` and `sw-cedefaults` and drops `lane_views.py` and its queue place. The prior probe called this residual and out of scope; it does not fix it, and the bug requires every conflicting glob with its holder or queue place.","digest":"02f64c0b00da3596","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/claim_guard_queued.py:24","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/claim_guard_queued.py:24"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"The rewritten queued refusal still calls `WG.queue_ahead` once and formats only that single `ahead`. Given an `update --globs` whose accepted globs are `brief.py` (queued behind `sw-cedefaults`) and `lane_views.py` (queued behind a different earlier waiter), `queued_globs(want, ahead.globs, cfg)` is filtered against whichever waiter `queue_ahead` returns first. The message names only that one glob and holder and then refuses the whole update (`The globs were not changed.`), so the other conflicting glob and its queue place are still omitted. The prior probe called this residual and out of scope; the new hunk did not change the single-ahead lookup, so the stated requirement to list every conflicting glob is still unmet on that input.","digest":"66629e7510573756","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/services/leases/lifecycle.py:plan_retarget","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/leases/lifecycle.py:plan_retarget"}],"chunks_total":2,"coverage":"2/2 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":1,"diff_chars":16015,"diff_sha":"9